To understand how this operation captures contextual dependencies across sequence elements, we can decompose the formula into four sequential mathematical steps.

### 1. Matrix Multiplication of Query ($Q$) and Transposed Key ($K^T$)
Given an input sequence of length $N$, we project the token representations into Query ($Q \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d_k}$), Key ($K \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d_k}$), and Value ($V \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d_v}$) matrices. The operation begins by multiplying $Q$ by the transpose of $K$:

$$S = Q K^T \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$$

Each entry $S_{i,j} = q_i \cdot k_j^T$ represents the raw, unscaled dot product between the query vector of token $i$ and the key vector of token $j$. Geometrically, this measures the degree of directional alignment and semantic correlation between two sequence positions.

### 2. Scaling by $\sqrt{d_k}$ to Mitigate Vanishing Gradients
As the key dimension $d_k$ grows, the magnitude of the dot products increases. Assuming components of $q_i$ and $k_j$ are independent random variables with zero mean and unit variance, their inner product yields a mean of 0 and a variance of $d_k$. Large input values push the subsequent $\text{softmax}$ function into regions with extremely small gradients, leading to saturation and vanishing gradients during backpropagation. Scaling the scores by $\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_k}}$ normalizes the variance back to 1:

$$\hat{S} = \frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}$$

### 3. Row-Wise Softmax Normalization
To convert scaled logits into valid probability distributions, we apply the $\text{softmax}$ function independently across each row $i$ of matrix $\hat{S}$:

$$A_{i,j} = \text{softmax}(\hat{S})_{i,j} = \frac{\exp(\hat{S}_{i,j})}{\sum_{m=1}^{N} \exp(\hat{S}_{i,m})}$$

The output matrix $A \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$ represents the attention weights, where each row sums to 1 ($\sum_j A_{i,j} = 1$). The value $A_{i,j}$ explicitly quantifies how much focus token $i$ assigns to token $j$.

### 4. Computing Contextual Output Representations via Value ($V$) Multiplication
Finally, we compute the output matrix $O \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d_v}$ by multiplying the normalized attention weights $A$ by the Value matrix $V$:

$$O = A V$$

For each position $i$, the output vector $o_i$ is a weighted linear combination of all value vectors in $V$, dynamic to the context of the entire sequence.

## Expanding to Multi-Head Attention for Richer Representations

Single-head scaled dot-product attention computes a single set of attention weights across the entire hidden dimension. However, this approach averages feature interactions, effectively smoothing over distinct contextual nuances. A single sequence position often needs to simultaneously capture multiple types of relationships—such as syntactic dependencies (e.g., subject-verb alignment) and long-range semantic associations (e.g., coreference resolution). Multi-Head Attention (MHA) overcomes this limitation by allowing the network to jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions.

To create these distinct subspaces, MHA linearly projects the input queries ($Q$), keys ($K$), and values ($V$) $h$ times using parameter matrices learned independently for each head $i$:

$$Q_i = Q W_i^Q, \quad K_i = K W_i^K, \quad V_i = V W_i^V$$

Here, $W_i^Q \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}} \times d_k}$, $W_i^K \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}} \times d_k}$, and $W_i^V \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}} \times d_v}$. Typically, the projection dimensions are set to $d_k = d_v = d_{\text{model}} / h$. By scaling down the per-head projection dimension, the computational complexity of MHA remains comparable to performing single-head attention over full $d_{\text{model}}$-dimensional vectors.

Once projected, scaled dot-product attention is executed in parallel across all $h$ heads:

$$\text{head}_i = \text{Attention}(Q_i, K_i, V_i) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{Q_i K_i^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V_i$$

Tensor libraries efficiently execute these parallel computations by reshaping input matrices into four-dimensional tensors of shape `[batch_size, num_heads, seq_len, head_dim]`, allowing accelerator hardware to compute attention maps for all heads in a single vectorized pass.

Finally, the output matrices $\text{head}_1, \dots, \text{head}_h$ (each of shape $[\text{seq\_len}, d_v]$) are concatenated along the feature dimension to restore the original feature dimension size ($h \cdot d_v = d_{\text{model}}$). This concatenated matrix is then projected through a learned output parameter matrix $W^O \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}} \times d_{\text{model}}}$:

$$\text{MultiHead}(Q, K, V) = \text{Concat}(\text{head}_1, \dots, \text{head}_h) W^O$$

